@subsection Representing Sequences

One of the useful structures we can build with pairs is a
@newterm{sequence}---an ordered collection of data objects.  There are, of
course, many ways to represent sequences in terms of pairs.  One particularly
straightforward representation is illustrated in @ref{Figure 2.4}, where the
sequence 1, 2, 3, 4 is represented as a chain of pairs.  The first component of
each pair is the corresponding item in the chain, and the second component of
the pair is the next pair in the chain.  The second component of the final pair
signals the end of the sequence by pointing to a distinguished value that is
not a pair, represented in box-and-pointer diagrams as a diagonal line and in
programs as the empty list written @code{[]}.  The entire sequence is
constructed by nested @code{::} operations:

@example ocaml
1 :: 2 :: 3 :: 4 :: []
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 2.4}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 2.4:} The sequence 1, 2, 3, 4 represented as a chain of pairs.

@example
