@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap2/Fig2.4e.std,,125mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 2.4:} The sequence 1, 2, 3, 4 represented as a chain of pairs.}
@end float

@noindent
Such a chain of pairs is called a @newterm{list}, and OCaml gives it a written
form: a list literal spells the elements between @code{[} and @code{]},
separated by semicolons.@footnote{In this book, we use @newterm{list} to mean a
chain of pairs terminated by the end-of-list marker.  In contrast, the term
@newterm{list structure} refers to any data structure made out of pairs, not
just to lists.}  The above sequence could be produced by
@code{[ 1; 2; 3; 4 ]}.  In general, the literal

@example ocaml
[ a₁; a₂; …; aₙ ]
@end example

@noindent
is equivalent to

@example ocaml
a₁ :: a₂ :: … :: aₙ :: []
@end example

@noindent
OCaml prints lists by printing the sequence of elements, separated by
semicolons and enclosed in brackets.  Thus, the data object in @ref{Figure 2.4}
is printed as @code{[1; 2; 3; 4]}:

@example ocaml
let one_through_four = [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ];;

one_through_four;;
(* => [1; 2; 3; 4] *)
@end example

@noindent
Be careful not to confuse the expression @code{[ 1; 2; 3; 4 ]} with the value
@code{[1; 2; 3; 4]}, which is the result obtained when the expression is
evaluated.  The confusion is harmless in OCaml only because code and data live
on opposite sides of the compiler: a list literal is data, never a procedure
call, so evaluating a printed list value would simply rebuild the same list.
Scheme must draw the sharper distinction because its printed list
@code{(1 2 3 4)} re-reads as an application---evaluating it would ``apply'' the
procedure @code{1} to arguments @code{2}, @code{3}, @code{4} and signal an
error.

We can think of @code{List.hd} as selecting the first item in the list, and of
@code{List.tl} as selecting the sublist consisting of all but the first item.
Nested applications of the two can be used to extract the second, third, and
subsequent items in the list.@footnote{Since nested applications of
@code{List.hd} and @code{List.tl} are cumbersome to write, Lisp dialects
provide abbreviations for them---for instance,

@example ocaml
(* cadr arg  =  List.hd (List.tl arg) *)
@end example

The names of all such procedures start with @code{c} and end with @code{r}.
Each @code{a} between them stands for a @code{car} operation and each @code{d}
for a @code{cdr} operation, to be applied in the same order in which they
appear in the name.  OCaml's standard library names the compound operations it
actually needs instead: @code{List.nth items n} returns the @math{n}-th item,
and the pattern @code{x :: rest} in a @code{match} names the first item and
the rest in one step, so compound @code{car}/@code{cdr} chains rarely arise.}
The constructor @code{::} makes a list like the original one, but with an
additional item at the beginning.

@example ocaml
List.hd one_through_four;;
(* => 1 *)

List.tl one_through_four;;
(* => [2; 3; 4] *)

List.hd (List.tl one_through_four);;
(* => 2 *)

10 :: one_through_four;;
(* => [10; 1; 2; 3; 4] *)

5 :: one_through_four;;
(* => [5; 1; 2; 3; 4] *)
@end example

@noindent
The value @code{[]}, used to terminate the chain of pairs, can be thought
of as a sequence of no elements, the @newterm{empty list}.  The word
@newterm{nil} is a contraction of the Latin word @emph{nihil}, which means
``nothing.''@footnote{It's remarkable how much energy in the standardization of
Lisp dialects has been dissipated in arguments that are literally over nothing:
Should @code{nil} be an ordinary name?  Should the value of @code{nil} be a
symbol?  Should it be a list?  Should it be a pair?  In Scheme, @code{nil} is
an ordinary name, which we use in this section as a variable whose value is the
end-of-list marker (just as @code{true} is an ordinary variable that has a true
value).  Other dialects of Lisp, including Common Lisp, treat @code{nil} as a
special symbol.  The authors of this book, who have endured too many language
standardization brawls, would like to avoid the entire issue, and OCaml lets
them: the end-of-list marker is the literal @code{[]}, a value of every list
type, with no binding of the name @code{nil} anywhere in the language.  Once we
have introduced quotation in @ref{2.3}, Scheme editions denote the empty list
as @code{'()}; the OCaml edition keeps writing @code{[]}.}

@subsubheading List operations

The use of pairs to represent sequences of elements as lists is accompanied by
conventional programming techniques for manipulating lists by successively
following the @code{::} chain down the list.  For example, the procedure
@code{list_ref} takes as arguments a list and a number @math{n} and returns the
@math{n^{\hbox{th}}} item of the list.  It is customary to number the elements of the list
beginning with 0.  The method for computing @code{list_ref} is the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
For @math{{n = 0}}, @code{list_ref} should return the first item of the list.

@item
Otherwise, @code{list_ref} should return  the @math{{(n - 1)}}-st item of the
rest of the list.

@end itemize

@example ocaml
let rec list_ref items n =
  match (items, n) with
  | first :: _, 0 -> first
  | _ :: rest, _ -> list_ref rest (n - 1)
  | [], _ -> invalid_arg "list_ref: index past the end"
;;

let squares = [ 1; 4; 9; 16; 25 ];;

list_ref squares 3;;
(* => 16 *)
@end example

@noindent
The first match arm plays the part of the @math{{n = 0}} case; the second strips
one item and counts down.  The third arm has no counterpart in the Scheme
definition: there, asking for an item past the end of the list tears a
@code{car} or @code{cdr} off of a non-pair and errors somewhere far from the
cause, while OCaml's @code{match} forces the end of the list to be a case that
is decided on purpose, and @code{invalid_arg} names the failure at the place
that understands it.  The standard library's @code{List.nth items n} is
@code{list_ref} with the same contract, raising @code{Invalid_argument} when
@math{n} falls outside the list.

Often we follow the chain down the whole list.  To aid in this, Scheme includes
a primitive predicate @code{null?}, which tests whether its argument is the
empty list; in OCaml the empty list is one arm of a @code{match}.  The
procedure @code{length}, which returns the number of items in a list,
illustrates this typical pattern of use:

@example ocaml
let rec length items =
  match items with
  | [] -> 0
  | _ :: cdr -> 1 + length cdr
;;

let odds = [ 1; 3; 5; 7 ];;

length odds;;
(* => 4 *)
@end example

@noindent
The @code{length} procedure implements a simple recursive plan. The reduction
step is:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{length} of any list is 1 plus the @code{length} of the rest of
the list.

@end itemize

@noindent
This is applied successively until we reach the base case:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The @code{length} of the empty list is 0.

@end itemize

@noindent
We could also compute @code{length} in an iterative style:

@example ocaml
let length_iter items =
  let rec iter a count =
    match a with
    | [] -> count
    | _ :: cdr -> iter cdr (1 + count)
  in
  iter items 0
;;
@end example

@noindent
The self-call is the entire result of @code{iter}, so OCaml compiles it to a
loop and the counter is the only thing that grows, exactly as in
@ref{1.2.1}'s iterative factorial.  The standard library's @code{List.length}
runs the same iterative plan.

Another conventional programming technique is to ``cons up'' an answer
list while following the chain down a list, as in the procedure @code{append},
which takes two lists as arguments and combines their elements to make a new
list:

@example ocaml
append squares odds;;
(* => [1; 4; 9; 16; 25; 1; 3; 5; 7] *)

append odds squares;;
(* => [1; 3; 5; 7; 1; 4; 9; 16; 25] *)
@end example

@noindent
@code{Append} is also implemented using a recursive plan.  To @code{append}
lists @code{list1} and @code{list2}, do the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
If @code{list1} is the empty list, then the result is just @code{list2}.

@item
Otherwise, @code{append} the rest of @code{list1} and @code{list2}, and
cons the first item of @code{list1} onto the result:

@end itemize

@example ocaml
let rec append list1 list2 =
  match list1 with
  | [] -> list2
  | car :: cdr -> car :: append cdr list2
;;
@end example

@noindent
The standard library's @code{List.append}, and its infix spelling @code{@},
are this @code{append}.

