@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.19}Exercise 2.19:} Consider the change-counting
program of @ref{1.2.2}.  It would be nice to be able to easily change
the currency used by the program, so that we could compute the number of ways
to change a British pound, for example.  As the program is written, the
knowledge of the currency is distributed partly into the procedure
@code{first_denomination} and partly into the procedure @code{cc}
(which knows that there are five kinds of U.S. coins).  It would be nicer to be
able to supply a list of coins to be used for making change.

We want to rewrite the procedure @code{cc} so that its second argument is a
list of the values of the coins to use rather than an integer specifying which
coins to use.  We could then have lists that defined each kind of currency:

@example ocaml
let us_coins = [ 50.0; 25.0; 10.0; 5.0; 1.0 ]

let uk_coins = [ 100.0; 50.0; 20.0; 10.0; 5.0; 2.0; 1.0; 0.5 ]
@end example

@noindent
Coin values are floats here because the British list carries a half-penny;
the United States list never holds a fractional value, but one type must
cover both.  We could then call @code{cc} as follows:

@example ocaml
cc 100.0 us_coins;;
(* => 292 *)
@end example

@noindent
To do this will require changing the program @code{cc} somewhat.  It will still
have the same form, but it will access its second argument differently, as
follows:

@example ocaml
let rec cc amount coin_values =
  if amount = 0.0 then 1
  else if amount < 0.0 || no_more coin_values then 0
  else
    (cc amount (except_first_denomination coin_values))
    + (cc (amount -. first_denomination coin_values) coin_values)
;;
@end example

@noindent
Define the procedures @code{first_denomination},
@code{except_first_denomination} and @code{no_more} in terms of primitive
operations on list structures.  Does the order of the list @code{coin_values}
affect the answer produced by @code{cc}?  Why or why not?
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.20}Exercise 2.20:} The procedures @code{+},
@code{*}, and @code{List.map} in Scheme take arbitrary numbers of arguments.
One way Scheme
defines such procedures is to use @code{define} with @newterm{dotted-tail notation}.
In a procedure definition, a parameter list that has a dot before
the last parameter name indicates that, when the procedure is called, the
initial parameters (if any) will have as values the initial arguments, as
usual, but the final parameter's value will be a @newterm{list} of any
remaining arguments.  For instance, given the definition

@example scheme
(define (f x y . z) ⟨body⟩)
@end example

@noindent
the procedure @code{f} can be called with two or more arguments.  If we evaluate
@code{(f 1 2 3 4 5 6)}, then in the body of @code{f}, @code{x} will be 1,
@code{y} will be 2, and @code{z} will be the list @code{(3 4 5 6)}.

OCaml has no dotted-tail notation: every function takes a fixed number of
arguments, fixed at its definition.  The idiom that plays the rest-argument
role is to take the initial arguments as usual and the remaining arguments as
one explicit list.  A two-or-more-argument @code{f} is written

@example ocaml
let f x y zs = (* ⟨body⟩, with [zs] holding the rest *) ⋯
@end example

@noindent
and called as @code{f 1 2 [ 3; 4; 5; 6 ]}, which binds @code{x} to 1,
@code{y} to 2, and @code{zs} to @code{[3; 4; 5; 6]}.@footnote{To define
@code{f} and @code{g} using @code{lambda}, Scheme would write

@example scheme
(define f (lambda (x y . z) ⟨body⟩))
(define g (lambda w ⟨body⟩))
@end example

OCaml's @code{fun} has no rest-argument form at all.  A function that accepts
zero or more arguments is written to take the whole list, so @code{g} becomes a
one-argument function over a list, called as @code{g [ 1; 2; 3; 4; 5; 6 ]}.}

Use this notation to write a procedure @code{same_parity} that takes one
integer and the list of the rest and returns a list of all the arguments that
have the same even-odd parity as the first argument.  For example,

@example ocaml
same_parity 1 [ 2; 3; 4; 5; 6; 7 ];;
(* => [1; 3; 5; 7] *)

same_parity 2 [ 3; 4; 5; 6; 7 ];;
(* => [2; 4; 6] *)
@end example
@end quotation

@subsubheading Mapping over lists

One extremely useful operation is to apply some transformation to each element
in a list and generate the list of results.  For instance, the following
procedure scales each number in a list by a given factor:

@example ocaml
let rec scale_list items factor =
  match items with
  | [] -> []
  | car :: cdr -> car * factor :: scale_list cdr factor
;;

scale_list [ 1; 2; 3; 4; 5 ] 10;;
(* => [10; 20; 30; 40; 50] *)
@end example

@noindent
We can abstract this general idea and capture it as a common pattern expressed
as a higher-order procedure, just as in @ref{1.3}.  The higher-order
procedure here is called @code{map}.  @code{Map} takes as arguments a procedure
of one argument and a list, and returns a list of the results produced by
applying the procedure to each element in the list:@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 78}
Scheme standardly provides a @code{map} procedure that
is more general than the one described here.  This more general @code{map}
takes a procedure of @math{n} arguments, together with @math{n} lists, and applies
the procedure to all the first elements of the lists, all the second elements
of the lists, and so on, returning a list of the results.  OCaml's library
@code{List.map} is the one-argument form only, and @code{List.map2} covers the
two-list case, demanding equal lengths and raising @code{Invalid_argument}
otherwise; there is no @math{n}-ary map for @math{n} above two.  For example:

@example ocaml
List.map2 ( + ) [ 1; 2; 3 ] [ 40; 50; 60 ];;
(* => [41; 52; 63] *)

List.map2 (fun x y -> x + (2 * y)) [ 1; 2; 3 ] [ 4; 5; 6 ];;
(* => [9; 12; 15] *)
@end example
}

@example ocaml
let rec map proc items =
  match items with
  | [] -> []
  | car :: cdr -> proc car :: map proc cdr
;;

map Float.abs [ -10.0; 2.5; -11.6; 17.0 ];;
(* => [10.; 2.5; 11.6; 17.] *)

map (fun x -> x * x) [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ];;
(* => [1; 4; 9; 16] *)
@end example

@noindent
Now we can give a new definition of @code{scale_list} in terms of @code{map}:

@example ocaml
let scale_list items factor = map (fun x -> x * factor) items
@end example

@noindent
@code{Map} is an important construct, not only because it captures a common
pattern, but because it establishes a higher level of abstraction in dealing
with lists.  In the original definition of @code{scale_list}, the recursive
structure of the program draws attention to the element-by-element processing
of the list.  Defining @code{scale_list} in terms of @code{map} suppresses that
level of detail and emphasizes that scaling transforms a list of elements to a
list of results.  The difference between the two definitions is not that the
computer is performing a different process (it isn't) but that we think about
the process differently.  In effect, @code{map} helps establish an abstraction
barrier that isolates the implementation of procedures that transform lists
from the details of how the elements of the list are extracted and combined.
Like the barriers shown in @ref{Figure 2.1}, this abstraction gives us the
flexibility to change the low-level details of how sequences are implemented,
while preserving the conceptual framework of operations that transform
sequences to sequences.  Section @ref{2.2.3} expands on this use of sequences
as a framework for organizing programs.  OCaml carries the barrier in its
standard library: @code{List.map} is this @code{map}, and the pipelines built
on it in @ref{2.2.3} read exactly the same against either one.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.21}Exercise 2.21:} The procedure @code{square_list}
takes a list of numbers as argument and returns a list of the squares of those
numbers.

@example ocaml
square_list [ 1; 2; 3; 4 ];;
(* => [1; 4; 9; 16] *)
@end example

@noindent
Here are two different definitions of @code{square_list}.  Complete both of
them by filling in the missing expressions:

@example ocaml
let rec square_list items =
  match items with
  | [] -> []
  | car :: cdr -> ⟨??⟩ :: square_list ⟨??⟩
;;

let square_list items = map ⟨??⟩ ⟨??⟩
@end example
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.22}Exercise 2.22:} Louis Reasoner tries to rewrite
the first @code{square_list} procedure of @ref{Exercise 2.21} so that it
evolves an iterative process:

@example ocaml
let square_list items =
  let rec iter things answer =
    match things with
    | [] -> answer
    | car :: cdr -> iter cdr (square car :: answer)
  in
  iter items []
;;
@end example

@noindent
Unfortunately, defining @code{square_list} this way produces the answer list in
the reverse order of the one desired.  Why?

Louis then tries to fix his bug by interchanging the two sides of the cons:

@example ocaml
let square_list items =
  let rec iter things answer =
    match things with
    | [] -> answer
    | car :: cdr -> iter cdr [ answer; square car ]
  in
  iter items []
;;
@end example

@noindent
This doesn't work either.  Explain.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.23}Exercise 2.23:} The procedure @code{for_each} is
similar to @code{map}.  It takes as arguments a procedure and a list of
elements.  However, rather than forming a list of the results, @code{for_each}
just applies the procedure to each of the elements in turn, from left to right.
The values returned by applying the procedure to the elements are not used at
all---@code{for_each} is used with procedures that perform an action, such as
printing.  For example,

@example ocaml
for_each
  (fun x -> print_newline (); print_int x)
  [ 57; 321; 88 ];;

(* 57 *)
(* 321 *)
(* 88 *)
(* => ok *)
@end example

@noindent
The value returned by the call to @code{for_each}, shown as @code{ok} above, is
something arbitrary, such as unit, OCaml's value of no information.  Give an
implementation of @code{for_each}.
@end quotation

@node	2.2.2, 2.2.3, 2.2.1, 2.2
@subsection Hierarchical Structures

The representation of sequences in terms of lists generalizes naturally to
represent sequences whose elements may themselves be sequences.  For example,
we can regard the object @code{((1 2) 3 4)} of Scheme as a list of three items,
the first of which is itself a list, @code{(1 2)}.  In OCaml, a list's elements
all carry one type, so a structure that mixes numbers and lists of numbers at
the same level gets its own @newterm{variant} type, with one constructor for a
leaf and one for the branching case:

@example ocaml
type tree =
  | Leaf of int
  | Node of tree list
@end example

@noindent
The object above is then the tree @code{Node [Node [Leaf 1; Leaf 2]; Leaf 3; Leaf 4]}:
a node holding three subtrees, the first of which is itself a node holding two
leaves.  This is the type system drawing @ref{Figure 2.5}'s structure: where
Scheme discovers at run time whether a @code{car} is a pair or a number, the
OCaml value says so in its constructor, and the compiler checks that every
program that takes the tree apart handles both cases.  @ref{Figure 2.5} shows
the representation of this structure in terms of pairs.

@float
@anchor{Figure 2.5}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 2.5:} Structure formed by @code{Node [Node [Leaf 1; Leaf 2]; Leaf 3; Leaf 4]},
the tree reading of Scheme's @code{(cons (list 1 2) (list 3 4))}.

@example
                                          (3 4)
                                            |
                                            V
((1 2) 3 4)  +---+---+                  +---+---+     +---+---+
        ---->| * | *-+----------------->| * | *-+---->| * | / |
             +-|-+---+                  +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+
               |                          |             |
               V                          V             V
      (1 2)  +---+---+     +---+---+    +---+         +---+
        ---->| * | *-+---->| * | / |    | 3 |         | 4 |
             +-|-+---+     +-|-+---+    +---+         +---+
               |             |
               V             V
             +---+         +---+
             | 1 |         | 2 |
             +---+         +---+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
