     /\\
    /  | \
(1 2)  3 4
 / \
 1 2
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap2/Fig2.6b,,40mm,Figure 2.6: The list structure in Figure 2.5 viewed as a tree.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap2/Fig2.6b.std,,40mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 2.6:} The list structure in @ref{Figure 2.5} viewed as a tree.}
@end float

@noindent
Recursion is a natural tool for dealing with tree structures, since we can
often reduce operations on trees to operations on their branches, which reduce
in turn to operations on the branches of the branches, and so on, until we
reach the leaves of the tree.  As an example, compare the @code{length}
procedure of @ref{2.2.1} with the @code{count_leaves} procedure, which
returns the total number of leaves of a tree.  Scheme's @code{x} is one value
playing two roles: a list, for @code{length}, and a tree, for
@code{count-leaves}.  OCaml keeps the two views apart: the tree and the list of
subtrees at its top level are two values, and each operation takes the one it
means:

@example ocaml
let x = Node [ Node [ Leaf 1; Leaf 2 ]; Leaf 3; Leaf 4 ]
@end example

@example ocaml
let x_top = [ Node [ Leaf 1; Leaf 2 ]; Leaf 3; Leaf 4 ];;

length x_top;;
(* => 3 *)
@end example

@example ocaml
count_leaves x;;
(* => 4 *)

let xx = [ x; x ];;

xx;;
(* => [Node [Node [Leaf 1; Leaf 2]; Leaf 3; Leaf 4]; Node [Node [Leaf 1; Leaf 2]; Leaf 3; Leaf 4]] *)

length xx;;
(* => 2 *)

count_leaves (Node xx);;
(* => 8 *)
@end example

@noindent
To implement @code{count_leaves}, recall the recursive plan for computing
@code{length}:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Length} of a list @code{x} is 1 plus @code{length} of the
rest of @code{x}.

@item
@code{Length} of the empty list is 0.

@end itemize

@noindent
@code{Count_leaves} is similar.  The value for the empty tree never comes up
here, because a @code{Node} carries the list of its subtrees and an empty
subtree list simply contributes nothing; but the leaf case is the base the
recursion lands on:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Count_leaves} of a leaf is 1.

@end itemize

@noindent
And in the branching case, where Scheme strips the @code{car} and the
@code{cdr} apart and recurses on each, we recurse on every subtree in the
list:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{Count_leaves} of a node is the sum of @code{count_leaves} over the
subtrees of the node.

@end itemize

@noindent
Here is the complete procedure:@footnote{In Scheme, the order of the first two
clauses in the @code{cond} matters, since the empty list satisfies
@code{null?} and also is not a pair.  The OCaml @code{match} has no such
fragility: a value matches exactly one constructor of its type, so there is no
clause order to get wrong, and the compiler objects if a constructor is left
out entirely.}

@example ocaml
let rec count_leaves tree =
  match tree with
  | Leaf _ -> 1
  | Node children ->
    List.fold_left (fun acc sub_tree -> acc + count_leaves sub_tree) 0 children
;;
@end example

