@end float

@noindent
By placing four copies of a @code{corner_split} appropriately, we obtain a
pattern called @code{square_limit}, whose application to @code{wave} and
@code{rogers} is shown in @ref{Figure 2.9}:

@example ocaml
let square_limit painter n =
  let quarter = corner_split painter n in
  let half = beside (flip_horiz quarter) quarter in
  below (flip_vert half) half
;;
@end example

@noindent
A note on order: the listings above name @code{beside}, @code{below},
@code{flip_vert}, @code{flip_horiz}, @code{wave}, and @code{rogers} before
anything that defines them.  A Scheme file tolerates that forward reference,
because nothing runs until every @code{define} above it has been read.  OCaml
resolves every name where it is written, so the runnable module of this section
walks the language bottom-up---vectors, frames, painters, then the
transformations and combiners, and only then these compound definitions---and
the subsections below follow that order.  Every value shown above is asserted
by the section's runner, which assembles the layers exactly in the order the
next three subsubsections present them.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.44}Exercise 2.44:} Define the procedure
@code{up_split} used by @code{corner_split}.  It is similar to
@code{right_split}, except that it switches the roles of @code{below} and
@code{beside}.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Higher-order operations

In addition to abstracting patterns of combining painters, we can work at a
higher level, abstracting patterns of combining painter operations.  That is,
we can view the painter operations as elements to manipulate and can write
means of combination for these elements---procedures that take painter
operations as arguments and create new painter operations.

For example, @code{flipped_pairs} and @code{square_limit} each arrange four
copies of a painter's image in a square pattern; they differ only in how they
orient the copies.  One way to abstract this pattern of painter combination is
with the following procedure, which takes four one-argument painter operations
and produces a painter operation that transforms a given painter with those
four operations and arranges the results in a square.  @code{tl}, @code{tr},
@code{bl}, and @code{br} are the transformations to apply to the top left copy,
the top right copy, the bottom left copy, and the bottom right copy,
respectively.

@example ocaml
let square_of_four tl tr bl br =
  fun painter ->
    let top = beside (tl painter) (tr painter) in
    let bottom = beside (bl painter) (br painter) in
    below bottom top
;;
@end example

@noindent
Then @code{flipped_pairs} can be defined in terms of @code{square_of_four} as
follows:@footnote{Equivalently, we could write

@example ocaml
let flipped_pairs = square_of_four Fun.id flip_vert Fun.id flip_vert
@end example
}

@example ocaml
let flipped_pairs painter =
  let combine4 =
    square_of_four Fun.id flip_vert Fun.id flip_vert
  in
  combine4 painter
;;
@end example

@noindent
and @code{square_limit} can be expressed as@footnote{@code{rotate_180} rotates a
painter by 180 degrees (see @ref{Exercise 2.50}).  Instead of @code{rotate_180}
we could say @code{(fun painter -> flip_vert (flip_horiz painter))}, using the
composition pattern of @ref{Exercise 1.42}.}

@example ocaml
let square_limit_of_four painter n =
  let combine4 =
    square_of_four flip_horiz Fun.id rotate_180 flip_vert
  in
  combine4 (corner_split painter n)
;;
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.45}Exercise 2.45:} @code{Right_split} and
@code{up_split} can be expressed as instances of a general splitting operation.
Define a procedure @code{split} with the property that evaluating

@example ocaml
let right_split = split beside below
let up_split = split below beside
@end example

@noindent
produces procedures @code{right_split} and @code{up_split} with the same
behaviors as the ones already defined.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Frames

Before we can show how to implement painters and their means of combination, we
must first consider frames.  A frame can be described by three vectors---an
origin vector and two edge vectors.  The origin vector specifies the offset of
the frame's origin from some absolute origin in the plane, and the edge vectors
specify the offsets of the frame's corners from its origin.  If the edges are
perpendicular, the frame will be rectangular.  Otherwise the frame will be a
more general parallelogram.

@ref{Figure 2.15} shows a frame and its associated vectors.
In accordance with data abstraction, we need not be specific yet about how
frames are represented, other than to say that there is a constructor
@code{make_frame}, which takes three vectors and produces a frame, and three
corresponding selectors @code{origin_frame}, @code{edge1_frame}, and
@code{edge2_frame} (see @ref{Exercise 2.47}).  The vectors themselves are
records with two float fields, and the listings of this subsection and the two
that follow are shown as they appear inside the section's @code{Picture}
module, where the vector and frame operations are in scope without their
module prefixes.

@float
@anchor{Figure 2.15}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 2.15:} A frame is described by three vectors
-- an origin and two edges.

@example
                         __
                     __--  \
                 __--       \
      __     __--            \   __
     |\  __--                 \__-|
       \-                  __--
frame   \              __--
edge2    \         __--    frame
vector    \    __--        edge1
           \_--            vector
            -   <--+
          frame    |
          origin   +-- (0, 0) point
          vector       on display screen
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap2/Fig2.15a,,78mm,Figure 2.15: A frame is described by three vectors --- an origin and two edges.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap2/Fig2.15a.std,,78mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 2.15:} A frame is described by three vectors --- an origin and two edges.}
@end float

@noindent
We will use coordinates in the unit square @math{{(0 \le x, y \le 1)}} to specify
images.  With each frame, we associate a @newterm{frame coordinate map}, which
will be used to shift and scale images to fit the frame.  The map transforms
the unit square into the frame by mapping the vector @math{{{\bf v} = (x, y)}} to
the vector sum
@displaymath
{\hbox{Origin(Frame)}} + {x \cdot \hbox{Edge}_1\hbox{(Frame)}} + {y \cdot \hbox{Edge}_2\hbox{(Frame)}.}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
Origin(Frame) + x * Edge_1(Frame) + y * Edge_2(Frame)
@end example

@end ifinfo
@noindent
For example, (0, 0) is mapped to the origin of the frame, (1, 1) to the vertex
diagonally opposite the origin, and (0.5, 0.5) to the center of the frame.  We
can create a frame's coordinate map with the following
procedure:@footnote{@code{Frame_coord_map} uses the vector operations described
in @ref{Exercise 2.46} below, which we assume have been implemented using some
representation for vectors.  Because of data abstraction, it doesn't matter
what this vector representation is, so long as the vector operations behave
correctly.}

@example ocaml
let frame_coord_map frame =
  fun v ->
    add_vect
      (origin_frame frame)
      (add_vect
         (scale_vect (xcor_vect v) (edge1_frame frame))
         (scale_vect (ycor_vect v) (edge2_frame frame)))
;;
@end example

@noindent
Observe that applying @code{frame_coord_map} to a frame returns a procedure
that, given a vector, returns a vector.  If the argument vector is in the unit
square, the result vector will be in the frame.  For example,

@example ocaml
frame_coord_map a_frame (make_vect 0.0 0.0) = origin_frame a_frame;;
(* => true *)
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.46}Exercise 2.46:} A two-dimensional vector @math{\bf v}
running from the origin to a point can be represented as a record with an
@math{x}-coordinate and a @math{y}-coordinate:

@example ocaml
type vect = { x : float; y : float }
@end example

@noindent
Implement a data abstraction for
vectors by giving a constructor @code{make_vect} and corresponding selectors
@code{xcor_vect} and @code{ycor_vect}.  In terms of your selectors and
constructor, implement procedures @code{add_vect}, @code{sub_vect}, and
@code{scale_vect} that perform the operations vector addition, vector
subtraction, and multiplying a vector by a scalar:
@displaymath
\eqalign{
	(x_1, y_1) + (x_2, y_2)  &=  (x_1 + x_2, y_1 + y_2), \cr
	(x_1, y_1) - (x_2, y_2)  &=  (x_1 - x_2, y_1 - y_2), \cr
	s \cdot (x, y) 		 &=  (sx, sy).
}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
(x_1, y_1) + (x_2, y_2) = (x_1 + x_2, y_1 + y_2)
(x_1, y_1) - (x_2, y_2) = (x_1 - x_2, y_1 - y_2)
             s * (x, y) = (sx, sy)
@end example
@end ifinfo
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.47}Exercise 2.47:} Here are two possible
constructors for frames:

@example ocaml
let make_frame origin edge1 edge2 = (origin, edge1, edge2)

let make_frame origin edge1 edge2 = (origin, (edge1, edge2))
@end example

@noindent
For each constructor supply the appropriate selectors to produce an
implementation for frames.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Painters

A painter is represented as a function that, given a frame as argument,
determines a particular image shifted and scaled to fit the frame.  That is to
say, if @code{p} is a painter and @code{f} is a frame, then @code{p f} is the
list of segments @code{p} draws in @code{f}.  Two types carry the idea:

@example ocaml
type segment = vect * vect

type painter = frame -> segment list
@end example

@noindent
A segment is a pair of the vectors running from the origin to its endpoints,
and a painter is a function from frames to segment lists.

The details of how primitive painters are implemented depend on the particular
characteristics of the graphics system and the type of image to be drawn.  This
edition renders SVG, so the lowest level of the language is a tiny module that
formats one segment as an SVG @code{<line>} element and wraps a list of them in
a document.  The document's @code{viewBox} names the rectangle of the plane the
canvas shows; computing it from the drawn segments---the smallest box that
contains them, grown by five percent on every side---lets the renderer frame
any painter, in any frame, without clipping it:

@example ocaml
module Svg = struct
  let line (a, b) =
    Printf.sprintf
      {|<line x1="%g" y1="%g" x2="%g" y2="%g"/>|}
      (xcor_vect a) (ycor_vect a) (xcor_vect b) (ycor_vect b)
  ;;

  let document ~view_box ~size body =
    Printf.sprintf
      {|<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="%d" height="%d" viewBox="%s"><g stroke="black" stroke-width="0.008" stroke-linecap="round" fill="none">%s</g></svg>|}
      size size view_box body
  ;;

  (* the smallest box containing [segments], grown five percent *)
  let view_box segments = ⟨computed as described in the text⟩
end
@end example

@noindent
With that in hand, we can create painters for line drawings, such as the
@code{wave} painter in @ref{Figure 2.10}, from lists of line segments as
follows:@footnote{@code{Segments_to_painter} uses the representation for line
segments described in @ref{Exercise 2.48} below.  It also returns its segments
in the order @code{List.map} visits them, in the spirit of the @code{for_each}
procedure described in @ref{Exercise 2.23}, which the Scheme original used to
draw its lines one at a time.}

@example ocaml
let segments_to_painter segment_list =
  fun frame ->
    let m = frame_coord_map frame in
    List.map
      (fun (start_v, end_v) -> (m start_v, m end_v))
      segment_list
;;
@end example

@noindent
The segments are given using coordinates with respect to the unit square.  For
each segment in the list, the painter transforms the segment endpoints with the
frame coordinate map; painting the frame returns the transformed segments
rather than drawing as a side effect, and the renderer turns them into an SVG
document:

@example ocaml
let render_to_string frame painter =
  let segments = painter frame in
  Svg.document
    ~view_box:(Svg.view_box segments)
    ~size:200
    (String.concat "" (List.map Svg.line segments))
;;

let write_svg ~path ~size frame painter =
  let document = render_to_string frame painter in
  let out = open_out path in
  output_string out document;
  close_out out
;;
@end example

@noindent
Representing painters as functions erects a powerful abstraction barrier in
the picture language.  We can create and intermix all sorts of primitive
painters, based on a variety of graphics capabilities. The details of their
implementation do not matter.  Any function can serve as a painter, provided
that it takes a frame as argument and determines something scaled to fit the
frame.@footnote{For example, the @code{rogers} painter of @ref{Figure 2.11} was
constructed in the original from a gray-level image: for each point in a given
frame, the painter determines the point in the image that is mapped to it under
the frame coordinate map, and shades it accordingly.  This edition's
@code{rogers} is a @code{segments_to_painter} drawing of a head and shoulders,
because the edition renders SVG lines only; the abstraction barrier is the
point, not the shading.  By allowing different types of painters, we are
capitalizing on the abstract data idea discussed in @ref{2.1.3}, where we
argued that a rational-number representation could be anything at all that
satisfies an appropriate condition.  Here we're using the fact that a painter
can be implemented in any way at all, so long as it determines something in the
designated frame.  @ref{2.1.3} also showed how pairs could be implemented as
procedures.  Painters are our second example of a procedural representation
for data.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.48}Exercise 2.48:} A directed line segment in the
plane can be represented as a pair of vectors---the vector running from the
origin to the start-point of the segment, and the vector running from the
origin to the end-point of the segment.  Use your vector representation from
@ref{Exercise 2.46} to define a representation for segments with a constructor
@code{make_segment} and selectors @code{start_segment} and @code{end_segment}.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 2.49}Exercise 2.49:} Use @code{segments_to_painter}
to define the following primitive painters:

@enumerate a

@item
The painter that draws the outline of the designated frame.

@item
The painter that draws an ``X'' by connecting opposite corners of the frame.

@item
The painter that draws a diamond shape by connecting the midpoints of the sides
of the frame.

@item
The @code{wave} painter.

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@subsubheading Transforming and combining painters

An operation on painters (such as @code{flip_vert} or @code{beside}) works by
creating a painter that invokes the original painters with respect to frames
derived from the argument frame.  Thus, for example, @code{flip_vert} doesn't
have to know how a painter works in order to flip it---it just has to know how
to turn a frame upside down: The flipped painter just uses the original
painter, but in the inverted frame.

Painter operations are based on the procedure @code{transform_painter}, which
takes as arguments a painter and information on how to transform a frame and
produces a new painter.  The transformed painter, when given a frame,
transforms the frame and calls the original painter on the transformed frame,
collecting its segments.  The arguments to @code{transform_painter} are points
(represented as vectors) that specify the corners of the new frame: When mapped
into the frame, the first point specifies the new frame's origin and the other
two specify the ends of its edge vectors.  Thus, arguments within the unit
square specify a frame contained within the original frame.

@example ocaml
let transform_painter painter origin corner1 corner2 =
  fun frame ->
    let m = frame_coord_map frame in
    let new_origin = m origin in
    painter
      (make_frame
         new_origin
         (sub_vect (m corner1) new_origin)
         (sub_vect (m corner2) new_origin))
;;
@end example

@noindent
Here's how to flip painter images vertically:

@example ocaml
let flip_vert painter =
  transform_painter
    painter
    (make_vect 0.0 1.0)   (* new origin *)
    (make_vect 1.0 1.0)   (* new end of edge1 *)
    (make_vect 0.0 0.0)   (* new end of edge2 *)
;;
@end example

@noindent
Using @code{transform_painter}, we can easily define new transformations.
For example, we can define a painter that shrinks its image to the
upper-right quarter of the frame it is given:

@example ocaml
let shrink_to_upper_right painter =
  transform_painter
    painter
    (make_vect 0.5 0.5)
    (make_vect 1.0 0.5)
    (make_vect 0.5 1.0)
;;
@end example

@noindent
Other transformations rotate images counterclockwise by 90
degrees@footnote{@code{rotate_90} is a pure rotation only for square frames,
because it also stretches and shrinks the image to fit into the rotated frame.}

@example ocaml
let rotate_90 painter =
  transform_painter
    painter
    (make_vect 1.0 0.0)
    (make_vect 1.0 1.0)
    (make_vect 0.0 0.0)
;;
@end example

@noindent
or squash images towards the center of the frame:@footnote{The diamond-shaped
images in @ref{Figure 2.10} and @ref{Figure 2.11} were created with
@code{squash_inwards} applied to @code{wave} and @code{rogers}.}

@example ocaml
let squash_inwards painter =
  transform_painter
    painter
    (make_vect 0.0 0.0)
    (make_vect 0.65 0.35)
    (make_vect 0.35 0.65)
;;
@end example

@noindent
Frame transformation is also the key to defining means of combining two or more
painters.  The @code{beside} procedure, for example, takes two painters,
transforms them to paint in the left and right halves of an argument frame
respectively, and produces a new, compound painter.  When the compound painter
is given a frame, it collects the first transformed painter's segments in the
left half of the frame and the second's in the right half, and returns them
together:

@example ocaml
let beside painter1 painter2 =
  let split_point = make_vect 0.5 0.0 in
  let paint_left =
    transform_painter
      painter1
      (make_vect 0.0 0.0)
      split_point
      (make_vect 0.0 1.0)
  in
  let paint_right =
    transform_painter
      painter2
      split_point
      (make_vect 1.0 0.0)
      (make_vect 0.5 1.0)
  in
  fun frame -> paint_left frame @ paint_right frame
;;
@end example

@noindent
Observe how the painter data abstraction, and in particular the representation
of painters as functions, makes @code{beside} easy to implement.  The
@code{beside} procedure need not know anything about the details of the
component painters other than that each painter will determine its segments in
its designated frame.

